# Carom — Balls, serving, and physics

This file defines how many balls are in play and how they are served, and how a
ball moves and interacts with the field: the physics loop, collision, and the
paddle bounce and spin mechanic. It builds on the geometry in
`specs/playfield.md` and the coordinate system in `specs/overview.md`. Every
figure below is exported from `src/constants.ts` under the name it is given here.

## The ball

One ball is in play, the ball from `specs/playfield.md`: radius `BALL_R`, serve
speed `SERVE_SPEED`, a `SPEED_MULT` multiplier per paddle hit up to the
`SPEED_CAP` cap, and the spin mechanic below.

## Physics

The engine hands the game the real elapsed time for each frame and imposes no
timestep of its own. Every rate in this specification — the speeds in px/s, the
spin acceleration in px/s^2, the spin decay per second, and the countdown in
seconds — is therefore integrated against the delta time the game is given, and
never against a count of frames. The outcome of a scenario does not depend on how
that elapsed time was divided into frames: the same interval delivered as one long
step, as many short ones, or as an uneven mixture reaches the same state. The core
is render-free: state advances purely from the elapsed time it is handed, with no
dependence on the canvas, on wall-clock time, or on the renderer. Any randomness
runs off the generator seeded from `state.rngState`. These properties let the game
be driven and reproduced from code, which `specs/instrumentation.md` relies on to
drive and inspect it.

Each frame, for the ball:

1. Apply the spin acceleration (below) to the velocity.
2. Advance position by `velocity * dt`.
3. Resolve collisions with walls, paddles, and obstacles.

## Collision

Resolve the ball against the top and bottom walls, the two paddles, and the two
obstacles. Treat the ball as a circle of radius `BALL_R` and each paddle as an
axis-aligned rectangle `PADDLE_W` by `PADDLE_H`.

- Top and bottom wall: reflect the vertical velocity (`vy -> -vy`) and push the
  ball back inside the field. Speed is unchanged.
- Obstacle: resolve the ball against the two obstacles as `specs/playfield.md`
  defines. An obstacle bounce leaves speed unchanged and preserves spin, so the
  spin keeps curving the ball after the bounce.
- Paddle: see the next section. A paddle hit is the only collision that changes
  speed and imparts spin.

The ball never passes through a paddle, wall, or obstacle, even at `SPEED_CAP`.

## Paddle bounce and spin

When the ball strikes a paddle:

1. Reflection angle from contact point. Let
   `offset = (ballCenterY - paddleCenterY) / PADDLE_HALF`, clamped to `[-1, 1]`.
   The outgoing angle from horizontal is `theta = offset * MAX_BOUNCE_ANGLE`
   (`55deg`). Hitting the paddle center sends the ball straight across; hitting
   the top or bottom edge sends it off at `MAX_BOUNCE_ANGLE`.

2. Speed. `speed = min(speed * SPEED_MULT, SPEED_CAP)`. The horizontal direction
   flips to point toward the opposing goal, giving new velocity
   `vx = +/- speed * cos(theta)` (sign toward the opponent) and
   `vy = speed * sin(theta)`.

3. Spin from paddle motion. The paddle's vertical velocity at contact adds spin:
   `spin += paddleVy * SPIN_FROM_PADDLE` (`0.85`), then clamp `spin` to
   `[-SPIN_CLAMP, SPIN_CLAMP]` (`+/-900`). A paddle moving downward as it strikes
   curves the ball one way, a paddle moving upward curves it the other, and a
   stationary paddle imparts no new spin.

Spin is a signed scalar carried by the ball, `state.ball.spin`. Each frame it
applies a lateral acceleration perpendicular to the ball's direction of travel, of
magnitude `|spin|` (in px/s^2), curving the path toward the side determined by the
sign of `spin`. Spin decays toward zero exponentially, losing half its magnitude
every `SPIN_HALFLIFE` (`0.8`) seconds (`spin *= 0.5 ^ (dt / SPIN_HALFLIFE)` per
frame). It persists across wall and obstacle bounces and changes only through
paddle hits and decay.

A paddle swiped at the moment of contact bends the ball's flight, letting a
player curve shots around the obstacles or wrong-foot the opponent.

## Serving

At the start of the match and after each point, the ball spawns at the field
center `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)` and holds there while `state.holdTimer` counts down
from `HOLD_TIME` (`1.0` second) to `0` — the Countdown screen in `specs/ui.md`,
rendered over the field. On the first frame the timer reaches `0`, the ball is
served at `SERVE_SPEED`:

- The serve travels toward `state.receiver`, the player who was just scored on.
  The first serve of a match always travels toward player one (`"left"`), so the
  match opens consistently.
- The serve leaves at `SERVE_ANGLE` (`12deg`) from horizontal, so the volley is
  never perfectly flat. Whether that angle points up or down the field is yours;
  either way the serve is within `SERVE_MAX_ANGLE` (`30deg`) of horizontal.

Nothing else launches the ball. `specs/instrumentation.md` ends the hold early by
setting `holdTimer` to `0`, and the serve that follows is this one.

## After a point

When a point is scored (a ball crosses a goal edge, per `specs/ui.md`), the rally
ends. The field holds through the countdown at the center, then the ball is
re-served toward the player who was just scored on.

